%[text] # OPT 212: Linear Algebra for Polynomial Fitting and Phase Recovery Coding Exercises
%[text] Solutions
%[text] This solution live script is written as a worked walkthrough. Each solution section starts with the physical or numerical idea, then the following code cell carries out that step. The recurring pattern is a least-squares model
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{f}\\approx \\mathbf{M}\\mathbf{a},$
%[text] where the columns of $\\mathbf{A}$ are known basis functions and the unknown vector $\\mathbf{c}$ contains the coefficients to estimate.
clear
close all
clc
format compact
format long g
set(groot,'DefaultAxesFontSize',14,'DefaultTextFontSize',14, ...
    'DefaultLegendFontSize', 11, 'DefaultColorbarFontSize', 1)
%[text] ## 
%[text] ## **Exercise 1:  General Polynomial Fitting**
%[text] ## **a.  Set up a model using known independent variables and coefficients**
%[text] This first activity is aimed at looking at a linear algebra approach to a polynomial fit.  The idea is that there are a series of known values of an independent variable x, and some function $f(x)$ that represents what is being measured.  Fitting requires a function that (usually) is based on a physically reasonable behavior.  For slowly varying phenomena, this is often a polynomial.   The model equation is 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(x)=a\_0+a\_1x+a\_2x^2,$
%[text] where we treat the a-coefficients as unknowns.  To set this up, we use a design, or measurement matrix:  
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{M}=\n \\left\[\\begin{array}{ccc}\n 1 & x\_1 & x\_1^2 \\\\\n 1 & x\_2 & x\_2^2 \\\\\n \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots \\\\\n 1 & x\_N & x\_N^2 \n \\end{array}\\right\].$
%[text] The design matrix has columns $1$, $x$, and $x^2$. Building those columns explicitly makes the connection to the polynomial easy to see. 
%[text] 
%[text] Begin by defining the coefficients and values of x.   Distribute x from -1 to 1 using a spacing between samples of .1.  Then plot the data.   
%[text] 
a0 = -0.3 %[control:slider:035e]{"position":[6,10]} %[output:4dcc3cd4]
a1 = -0.4 %[control:slider:7aec]{"position":[6,10]} %[output:65b93343]
a2 = 0.5 %[control:slider:23ac]{"position":[6,9]} %[output:768f2c95]
xx = -1:0.1:1;
ff = a0 + a1.*xx + a2.*xx.^2;

% Plot the results
figure; %[output:4ef64298]
plot(xx, ff,'xr')  %[output:4ef64298]
xlabel('x'); %[output:4ef64298]
ylabel('y'); %[output:4ef64298]
ylim([-1 1]) %[output:4ef64298]
title('Quadratic Function Plot'); %[output:4ef64298]
grid on; %[output:4ef64298]
%[text] 
%[text] ## **b.  Fit to the data**
%[text] We know want to fit to the data.    Put the independent variables in a matrix  $\\mathbf{M}=\n \\left\[\\begin{array}{ccc}\n 1 & x\_1 & x\_1^2 \\\\\n 1 & x\_2 & x\_2^2\\\\\n \\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots \\\\\n 1 & x\_N & x\_N^2 \n \\end{array}\\right\]$,  
%[text] Put the values of $\\mathbf{a}=\n \\left\[\\begin{array}{cccc}\n a\_0\\\\a\_1\\\\a\_2\\\\\n \\end{array}\\right\].$  and $\\mathbf{f}=\n \\left\[\\begin{array}{cccc}\n f\_0\\\\f\_1\\\\f\_2\\\\\n \\vdots \\\\\n f\_N\n \\end{array}\\right\]\n \\end{array}\\right\].$  into column vectors.  
%[text] then $\\mathbf{f}=\\mathbf{M}\\mathbf{a}$. 
%[text] Fit to the data by inverting the matrix equation .... 
% Turn xx and ff into column vectors
x_col = xx';
f_col = ff';

% Insert code here -- define the measurement matrix with one line of code
M = x_col.^[0 1 2];
% Estimate the coefficients a 
a_measured = M\f_col %[output:35fb7546]
%[text] Now use the coefficients to calculate the fitted curve using a new measurement matrix that has many more points. To do so, define a new independent variable xf (for fitting) that uses many more points (I'd suggest 201) over the same interval as your data.   
%[text] Then define a function based on the calculated a-coefficients that you can compare, in a plot, with the data.  Since this data is exact, the fit should look perfect.   
xf = linspace(-1,1,201)';
%M_fit = xf.^[0 1 2];
f_fit = a_measured(1) + a_measured(2).*xf + a_measured(3).*xf.^2;
% Plot the results
hold on %[output:4ef64298]
figure(2); %[output:5296cdf3]
plot(xx, ff,'xr'); hold on %[output:5296cdf3]
plot(xf,f_fit,'-r','LineWidth',2) %[output:5296cdf3]
xlabel('x'); %[output:5296cdf3]
ylabel('y'); %[output:5296cdf3]
ylim([-1 1]) %[output:5296cdf3]
title('Quadratic Function Plot'); %[output:5296cdf3]
grid on; %[output:5296cdf3]


%[text] 
%[text] ## **c.  Add Gaussian noise, re-fit and re-plot**
%[text] Now add  some Gaussian random noise using the randn function and plot.  Define a noise level to multiply by the randn function.       
noise_level = 0.05; %[control:slider:058f]{"position":[15,19]}
f_col = f_col + noise_level.*randn(size(f_col));
a_noisy = M\f_col;
f_fit = a_noisy(1) + a_noisy(2).*xf + a_noisy(3).*xf.^2;
% Plot the results
figure(2);
plot(xx, f_col,'xr'); hold on %[output:5296cdf3]
plot(xf,f_fit,'-b','LineWidth',2) %[output:5296cdf3]
xlabel('x'); %[output:5296cdf3]
ylabel('y'); %[output:5296cdf3]
ylim([-1 1]) %[output:5296cdf3]
title('Quadratic Function Plot'); %[output:5296cdf3]
grid on; %[output:5296cdf3]



%[text] 
%[text] ## Exercise 2:Phase-Shifting Interferometry
%[text] Phase-shifting interferometry measures several intensity frames with a known reference phase shift. The intensity model at each pupil point is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I\_k=a+b\\cos(\\phi+\\delta\_k).$
%[text] Expanding the cosine gives a linear system
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I\_k=p\_1+p\_2\\cos\\delta\_k-p\_3\\sin\\delta\_k,$
%[text] where
%[text]{"align":"center"} $p\_1=a,\\qquad p\_2=b\\cos\\phi,\\qquad p\_3=b\\sin\\phi.$
%[text] After solving for $p\_1$, $p\_2$, and $p\_3$, you can recover the phase from
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\phi=\\it{atan2}(p\_3,p\_2).$
%[text] Tasks:
%[text] 1\. Define values for a and b and the phase phi (in radians) (b must be less than or equal to a since this is an irradiance measurement and has to be non-negative).     
%[text] 2\. Define an column vector of seven values for the phase steps $\\delta$ and a test value for the phase $\\phi$.  
%[text] 3\. Define an irradiance column vector that represents the irradiance measurements as the phase step is varied. Create a plot showing discrete points.  Now add to that irradiance a noise level similar to what is done above.    
%[text] 4\. Assemble a `7 x 3` measurement (design) matrix for the known phase steps using a column of ones, a column of all of the values of $cos\\delta$ and all of the values of $-sin\\delta$.   
%[text] 5\. Compute the value of $\\mathbf{p}$ using the $\\mathbf{p}=\\mathbf{M}\\backslash\\mathbf{I}$ inversion.  
%[text] 6\. Recover the phase using the atan2 function.     
%[text] 7\. Create a 101 point array of delta values, and use the components  of $\\mathbf{p}$ to reconstruct the irradiance I. Add this to your plot showing the $\\delta$ data points.  
%[text] ## Exercise 3 Work Area
%[text] TODO: Add your code for Exercise 3 here.
a = 1;  % OK to put a control here
b = 0.8;  % needs to be less than a
noise_level = 0.15;  % add a noise level %[control:slider:3f7d]{"position":[15,19]}
phi_test = 1;  % test value of phi
delta = linspace(0,2*pi,7)'; % create an array and make it a column vector
% Create perfect and noisy measured irradiances
I_perfect = a + b*cos(phi_test + delta);
I_noisy = I_perfect + noise_level*randn(size(I_perfect)) %[output:86ebb03f]

% Now plot
figure %[output:61340307]
plot(delta, I_perfect, 'ob') %[output:61340307]
hold on %[output:61340307]
plot(delta,I_noisy,'xb') %[output:61340307]
xlabel('\delta (radians)') %[output:61340307]
ylabel('Irradiance') %[output:61340307]
grid on %[output:61340307]

% create the measurement matrix, starting with a column of ones.
M = [ones(size(delta)), cos(delta), sin(delta)];



% Hint:  check the size of the measurement matrix to make sure it is right
size(M) %[output:08d2ca70]


% compute the vector p using the noisy Irradiance
p = M\I_noisy %[output:4e204bf3]

% recover the phase and compare
phi_measured = atan2(-p(3),p(2)) %[output:22bbcefe]

% create Irradiance function based on the fitted value of delta
delta_fit = linspace(0,2*pi,101)';
M_fit = [ones(size(delta_fit)), cos(delta_fit), sin(delta_fit)];
I_fit = M_fit*p;

I_perfect_fit = a + b*cos(phi_test + delta_fit);

plot(delta_fit,I_perfect_fit,'-r') %[output:61340307]
plot(delta_fit,I_fit,'-b') %[output:61340307]

% add to the plot




%[text] For a noise level of 0.2 values of a=1 and b = .8, you should see something like this.  The red line is the 'perfect' phase measurement; the blue is the fit based on the noisy data.   
%[text] ![](text:image:6591)
%[text] 
%[text] 
%[text] ## Exercise 3 Discussion
%[text] Trying different noise levels and looking at the differences between the test and measured phase, how much noise do you think is tolerable for a 'good' measurement?  Add this as some comments/discussions below.  
%[text] 
%[text] Creating a slider for the noise variable, I tested different values of noise and found that up to around 0.15 is where I believe the noise is tolerable for a 'good' measurement. At these levels, the fitted curve remains close to the perfect curve and the measured phase remains reasonably close to phi\_test. I think a reasonable phase error is about 0.1 radians (10% error) and noise of 0.15 consistently stays under that.
%[text] 

